// **Households that provision themselves**: `bake` and `eat` that read the actor's own shelf (`Input::Stock`).
//
// This file is an overlay, not an addition. It holds the two activities the starter pack already defines, in a
// form that closes the loop from "my shelf is bare" to "make bread", and a host that wants it **replaces** the
// starter's `bake` and `eat` with these by id (`merge` needs disjoint ids, so it cannot do this):
//
//     let mut file = DefsFile::starter()?;
//     let overlay = DefsFile::from_ron(include_str!("assets/provisioning.society.ron"))?;
//     for activity in overlay.activities {
//         let slot = file.activities.iter_mut().find(|a| a.id == activity.id);
//         *slot.expect("the starter pack has this activity") = activity;
//     }
//     let defs = SocietyDefs::compile(file)?;
//
// `docs/loop/runs/2026-10-30-28/` has the measurement. In one line: a town of 60 on the starter pack's clock-driven
// `bake` loses its bread (86 % of person-ticks at the floor on `food` by game-day 12, bread 0.05 a head, 0 to 1 bakes
// a day), because a one-hour dawn window and a draw against nine other options do not scale with the population;
// with this overlay the same town holds 1.4 loaves a head and 23 % at the floor over its last six game-days (16-43 % on
// any one day), with 0-13 bakes a day answering to the shelves.
(
    activities: [
        (
            // **Make bread when hungry and the shelf is bare.** The appetitive act that precedes the consummatory
            // one: "animals are usually separated from motivational stimuli by distance and time, they must
            // allocate considerable resources toward stimulus-seeking behavior ... 'preparatory' (appetitive)
            // actions leading to 'consummatory' events" (Woodworth 1918 and Konorski 1976, in the corpus doc
            // `10.1002/hipo.22488`). Eating is the consummatory act; with nothing to eat, the act that follows
            // hunger is the one that makes food.
            //
            // **A balancing loop closes on a stock.** The starter pack's `bake` works by the clock, which is an
            // open loop: it is stable only if the clock's output happens to equal what the village eats, and a
            // town of sixty on a pack tuned at twenty-four does not (Zunic's production-consumption model: "if the
            // parameters are chosen such that the inflow of resources into the system equals the outflow, then the
            // economy is stable ... by upsetting this balance we can simulate ... shortages", corpus doc
            // `eth-cgl-ar_games-Zun16a`; Meadows, *Thinking in Systems*, on balancing loops). Each household here
            // tops up its own shelf, so production is the sum of what the shelves ask for and **scales with the
            // population by construction**.
            //
            // The terms, in order. `Hour` is daylight (a wider window than the starter's dawn hour, because a
            // decision has to land inside it: a sleeper that went to bed late wakes at 07:30 and never meets
            // `[6, 7)`). `Skill` is as before. `Stock` is `1.0` on an empty shelf and `0.0` from one loaf (a day's
            // supply a head, `Tuning::price_full_store`) up, so a household with bread has nothing to make: the
            // input is clamped to `[0, 1]`. `Need("food")` is the hunger that makes bare shelves matter: a
            // logistic that is about 0.5 at half-fed and rises as the person gets hungrier, so a household with a
            // bare shelf and a full stomach is not made to bake at once.
            //
            // The curve centres and the daylight window are design defaults, measured: `docs/loop/runs/2026-10-30-28/`.
            id: "bake",
            target: Place("food"),
            duration_hours: 1.0,
            // **Urgent while hungry** (`urgent`, below): a household that is hungry and has nothing on the shelf is in
            // the same tier as `eat`, so the bread gets made before the play, the work and the chatter. Without this
            // line `eat` alone takes the tier, and on a bare shelf it scores a fourteenth, so `tier_cutoff` drops it
            // and `bake` is the only option left in the tier: the two lines are one design, and `bake` is in the
            // bucket because it is the act that solves the hunger when the shelf is empty, though it `satisfies`
            // nothing itself.
            urgent: Some((need: "food", below: 0.25, rank: 1)),
            produces: [("bread", 4.0)],
            considerations: [
                (input: Hour, curve: Hours(6.0, 20.0)),
                (input: Skill("craft"), curve: Linear(1.0, 0.0)),
                (input: Stock("bread"), curve: Linear(-1.0, 1.0)),
                (input: Need("food"), curve: Logistic(-10.0, 0.5)),
            ],
            satisfies: [],
            interaction: None,
            barks: ["Fresh loaves."],
        ),
        (
            // **Eat where there is something to eat.** *The Sims*' utility scores "very hungry" together with "the
            // availability of poor food" (Game AI Pro, "Utility Systems", corpus doc `Game AI Pro Collected
            // Wisdom`), and a smart object advertises an affordance only when its precondition on the user's state
            // holds (Zun15a, `eth-cgl-ar_games-Zun15a`). The starter's `eat` scores hunger alone, so an empty-handed
            // person walks to a table that cannot feed them (measured: 100-140 meals a day started in a town of
            // sixty with no bread anywhere).
            //
            // `Stock` reads a quarter of a loaf, one meal (`consumes` below), as `1.0`. It is **not a gate**:
            // the floor is `0.05`, "almost never" in the scoring's own words (a definition that wants "almost
            // never" must say so; `0` is final), so an empty shelf prices the meal at about a fourteenth rather than
            // forbidding it, which keeps `economy.rs`'s rule that having bread changes what a person prefers and
            // never what they may do.
            id: "eat",
            target: Place("food"),
            duration_hours: 0.75,
            // **Hunger takes precedence** (Maslow 1943, DOI 10.1037/h0054346: "the most prepotent goal will
            // monopolize consciousness ... The less prepotent needs are minimized"). Under `food` 0.25 a meal is in
            // the tier above work, craft and company; the Sims' need buckets are the same mechanism ("a starving Sim
            // will never even consider watching TV unless they fail to find anything that can solve their hunger",
            // Game AI Pro 1 ch. 9), and dual-utility ranks that follow the situation are Dill's (Game AI Pro 2 ch. 3).
            // Rank 1 is `sleep`'s: a tired, hungry person at night draws between the two by score.
            //
            // **It is only safe with the line above and the shelf term below.** On the starter pack's `eat` (no
            // shelf term, and a `bake` outside the bucket) the same line empties the shelves and the floor rises
            // from 19 to 27 % at 0.25 and 52 % at 0.35 (`docs/loop/runs/2026-10-30-29/research.md`): the bucket was full
            // of an option that cannot solve the hunger. `0.25` is a design default, swept at 0.15 / 0.25 / 0.35.
            urgent: Some((need: "food", below: 0.25, rank: 1)),
            considerations: [
                (input: Need("food"), curve: Logistic(-10.0, 0.35)),
                (input: Stock("bread"), curve: Linear(4.0, 0.05)),
            ],
            satisfies: [("food", 1.2)],
            consumes: [("bread", 0.25)],
            interaction: None,
            barks: ["Mmm, bread."],
        ),
    ],
)
